= Blood pressure — Technical =

== Measurement model ==
Routine arterial pressure readings estimate systolic and diastolic pressure from a peripheral artery. Cuff-based auscultatory and oscillometric methods differ in how they infer pressure; oscillometric algorithms can vary among devices. Reported results depend on cuff dimensions, calibration, posture, rest, movement and measurement schedule.

The pressure waveform changes over each cardiac cycle. The notation systolic/diastolic expresses extremes of the pulsatile arterial waveform rather than a single time-invariant scalar.

== Hemodynamic interpretation ==
A simplified steady-flow relation connects pressure difference, flow and resistance:

<pre>
pressure difference ≈ flow × resistance
</pre>

This approximation is useful for lumped hemodynamic models, but arteries are compliant, pressure is pulsatile and flow can be unsteady. Systolic or diastolic pressure cannot be determined from resistance alone. A simplified mean arterial pressure estimate from the two numbers is only an approximation, especially when heart rate and waveform shape differ.

== Evidence and limitations ==
Repeated measurements are usually required to distinguish persistent elevation from situational changes. Individual diagnostic thresholds, treatment choices and measurement schedules belong to current patient- and jurisdiction-specific clinical guidance, not to a generic physical definition.

